.nh
.TH crun 1 "User Commands"

.SH NAME
krun \- crun based OCI runtime using libkrun to run containerized programs in
isolated KVM environments


.SH SYNOPSIS
krun [global options] command [command options] [arguments...]


.SH DESCRIPTION
krun is a sub package of the crun command line program for running Linux
containers that follow the Open Container Initiative (OCI) format. The krun
command is a symbolic link to the crun executable, that tells crun to run in
krun mode.

.PP
krun uses the dynamic libkrun library to run processes in an isolated
environment using KVM Virtualization.

.PP
libkrun integrates a VMM (Virtual Machine Monitor, the userspace side of a
Hypervisor) with the minimum amount of emulated devices required for its
purpose, abstracting most of the complexity from Virtual Machine management.

.PP
Because of the additional isolation, sharing content with processes and other
containers outside of the krun VM is more difficult.


.SH DISK CONFIGURATION
krun can attach additional raw disk images to the libkrun microVM as
virtio-block data disks. Disk configuration is additive: it does not replace
the root disk, change virtiofs, mount filesystems inside the guest, or support
hotplug after the VM has started.

.PP
Disk annotations use a contiguous zero-based index:

.EX
krun.disk.0.path=/path/to/nix.raw
krun.disk.0.id=nix
krun.disk.0.readonly=false
.EE

.PP
The supported fields are:

.IP \[bu] 2
\fBkrun.disk.N.path\fR: path to the raw disk image. This field is required.
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBkrun.disk.N.id\fR: block device identifier passed to libkrun. This field is required.
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBkrun.disk.N.readonly\fR: optional read-only flag. The default is \fBfalse\fR.

.PP
The disk image path must point to an existing non-empty regular file. When \fBreadonly\fR is \fBfalse\fR, crun must be able to open the image read-write; use \fBreadonly=true\fR for images that should only be opened read-only.

.PP
\fBN\fR must be \fB0\fR or a canonical decimal number without a sign or leading zeroes. Indexes must be contiguous from \fB0\fR. The annotation \fBreadonly\fR value must be exactly \fBtrue\fR or \fBfalse\fR.

.PP
The equivalent \fB.krun_vm.json\fR form is:

.EX
{
  "disks": [
    {
      "path": "/path/to/nix.raw",
      "id": "nix",
      "readonly": false
    }
  ]
}
.EE

.PP
If any \fBkrun.disk.N.*\fR annotation is present, the annotation disk list overrides the \fB.krun_vm.json\fR \fBdisks\fR list entirely. Other top-level \fB.krun_vm.json\fR settings keep their existing behavior.

.PP
Only raw disk images are supported through this interface. The libkrun \fBformat\fR, \fBdirect_io\fR, and \fBsync_mode\fR options are intentionally not exposed. Extra disks are rejected for the \fBsev\fR flavor because that path configures a root disk with \fBkrun_set_root_disk\fR, which is mutually exclusive with \fBkrun_add_disk\fR. Unsupported fields in the \fBkrun.disk.N.*\fR annotation namespace or inside \fB.krun_vm.json\fR \fBdisks\fR entries are rejected.


.SH COMMANDS
See crun.1 man page for the commands available to krun


.SH SEE ALSO
crun.1
